#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# dev/stabilize-canary — the setup of THE SMALL CANARY of the stabilization workflow's
# `test` stage (DECISIONS.md -> 2026-10-07, "The small canary's setup"; the second repair
# plan's task K7 and its section 6, "The small canary",
# completions/artifacts/M55/stabilization-build/re-review-test-half/plan.md).
#
# No `test` stage has run under the Workflow runtime with real agents. The small canary
# is that run, once, before the real run is opened: ONE invocation of the stage, from its
# first step to its pushed record, and then one more to see what an invocation answers
# that has nothing to do. This tool builds the place it runs in, where NOTHING OF IT CAN
# REACH THIS REPOSITORY OR ITS REMOTE, and prints — as one line — what is then invoked.
#
# WHAT A SETUP MAKES, all of it under ONE root outside the repository:
#
#   ROOT/origin.git    a bare repository: THE CLONE'S ONE REMOTE
#   ROOT/clone         a repository of its own that holds the commit named and the
#                      previous release's commit, fetched from this one BY PATH — so no
#                      remote of it ever named this repository, and none names this
#                      repository's remote. Checked out: the loop branch `fix/RUN`, which
#                      exists there and nowhere else, with ONE commit on the commit named:
#   ROOT/clone/completions/artifacts/RUN/
#                      THE SYNTHETIC OPENING (below), written through the clone's own
#                      dev/stabilize-record, committed, and pushed to ROOT/origin.git by
#                      the clone's own dev/stabilize-step — vetted, as every push of a
#                      run is
#   ROOT/scratch       the scratch root the invocation is handed
#   ROOT/results       where the session that invokes the stage writes what each
#                      invocation returned (`returns`, below) — whoever starts it
#   ROOT/canary.json   the line this setup printed, kept: what `check` reads the run from
#   ROOT/prompts       under --parts only: ONE PROMPT FILE FOR EACH ACT of a run in parts
#                      (THE ACTS OF A RUN IN PARTS, below)
#
# Usage — every act prints ONE line of JSON on stdout, whatever it exits with:
#
#   dev/stabilize-canary setup --root ROOT --commit REV --run RUN --previous VERSION
#                        --previous-commit SHA [--items ID,ID]
#                        [--parts [--budget N] [--order CLASS,CLASS]]
#       ROOT is an ABSOLUTE path of plain segments (the harness takes no other scratch
#       root) — never a relative one, which would be read from wherever the caller
#       stands. It is not there yet and its parent is, or it is an empty directory, as
#       `mktemp -d` mints one: a root that holds anything is refused (exists), a link
#       planted in it too, so a root is used once. It lies OUTSIDE THE REPOSITORY, judged
#       on its real path: in no working tree of it, the main one's git directory
#       included (inside). REV is the candidate: any name this repository resolves to a
#       commit (no-commit) that holds the two scripts of a run and the harness (unfit) —
#       the COMMITTED bytes, never the working tree. RUN is a slug that this repository does
#       not hold: no completions/artifacts/RUN in the tree or in the commit, and no
#       branch `fix/RUN` here or on a remote (taken) — so a stage launched from the wrong
#       directory finds no such run and halts at its first step with nothing written.
#       VERSION and SHA are the previous release and the commit it was released from (a
#       full sha this repository holds): what the preflight builds the second binary
#       from. --items names the opening's items (THE SYNTHETIC OPENING); without it, the
#       five that are marked there. --parts is THE SWITCH: with it the setup writes
#       THE OPENING OF A RUN IN PARTS (below) in place of the synthetic opening — a run
#       with a queue — and --items names items of THAT opening's table; --budget and
#       --order are that opening's, each with its default there, and neither is taken
#       without the switch (usage). The commit named must then also hold, in its
#       DECISIONS.md, exactly one entry that states the exit rule (unfit).
#       Everything is checked before anything is made; a setup that fails after that
#       leaves a root that holds no canary.json, which no act takes for a canary. It ends
#       with `check`, and with the state a fresh run has — `next` is `test`, round 1,
#       attempt 1, the opening owing nothing — or it refuses (not-fresh).
#       Prints {status: ready, root, clone, origin, scratch, results, run, loop, run_dir,
#       candidate, head, remote_head, previous: {version, commit}, door, items: [{item,
#       kind}], seeded, state: {next, round, attempt}, session_cwd, script_path, args,
#       args_then, returns: {first, then}, check, expect: {agents, tool_steps,
#       wall_minutes}} — WHAT THE ORCHESTRATOR INVOKES, below. UNDER --parts the line
#       holds no `seeded`, `args`, `args_then`, `returns` or `expect` — those are the
#       small canary's one stage — and holds instead, after `items`: plants: [{key,
#       class}], condition, budget, hand_on, order; and, after `state`: queue — the keys
#       the run's queue ranks, first to last — and listed: {WHY: [key]}, every row that
#       is in no queue under the word that keeps it out, as the run's own state says
#       both; and, after `script_path`: prompts — the directory of the prompt files —
#       acts: [{act, args, prompt, return, expect [, human] [, verdicts]}], one for each
#       act of the parts, in the order they are made (THE ACTS OF A RUN IN PARTS) — and
#       tool: {sha256, as_committed}: the sha256 of THE FILE THAT RAN, which is the
#       working tree's, and whether it is the file the commit named holds at this
#       tool's path — the one the clone holds, whose header an agent there can read.
#
#   dev/stabilize-canary check --root ROOT
#       THE CLONE CAN REACH NOTHING BUT ITS BARE REMOTE — asserted, before the session
#       starts and whenever it is asked — and what the run holds, read. ROOT holds a
#       canary (missing), and each of its directories is that directory and no link
#       (link). The clone has ONE remote, `origin`; what git answers that it fetches from
#       and pushes to — under the configuration THE CALLER runs with, so a rewrite in the
#       session's own configuration is seen — is ROOT/origin.git; and the clone's own
#       configuration holds no `url.*` rewrite and names, anywhere in it, no remote of
#       this repository and not this repository (not-isolated). It writes nothing but what
#       `git status` writes in the CLONE.
#       Prints {status: checked, root, run, loop, branch, remotes, fetch_url, push_url,
#       head, remote_head, pushed, records, pending, state: {next, round, attempt [,
#       refused]}, not_ready, items: [{item, kind, selected}], ledger: [{key, grade,
#       disposition, route}], returned: {first, then}} — `records` is how many commits
#       lie on the opening, `pending` how many lines `git status --porcelain` has,
#       `pushed` whether the bare repository stands at the clone's head, `state` what
#       the run's own state says a `test` stage would work on, `returned` whether each
#       file of `returns` is there.
#       A WRITE OF THE RUN THAT WAS KILLED BETWEEN ITS FILES IS A STATE, AND IS READ AS
#       ONE: the record script does not read the run's state across it (its refusal
#       `interrupted`), and `check` then exits 0 all the same — `state` is {next: null,
#       round: null, attempt: null, refused: interrupted}, after it stands interrupted:
#       {said: that script's own line, then: what follows — the next invocation of the
#       stage finishes the write by itself}, and what the state alone could say is
#       empty or null. NO REFUSAL OF A TOOL `check` CALLS — git, the clone's record
#       script — IS EVER TURNED INTO *the root holds no canary*: such a refusal is
#       `tool`, and its recommendation says that the canary stands as it stood and
#       that no setup is asked for. Only a root with no canary.json holds none (missing).
#       FOR A ROOT OF THE PARTS — one a setup under --parts made — the line holds no
#       `returned`, whose two files no act of the parts writes, and holds instead, after
#       `ledger`: budget: {limit, spent, left} · queue: the keys the queue ranks ·
#       listed: {WHY: [key]} · human: [{key, why}], the human's list · filed: [{file,
#       key, verdict, attempt, sha256, counts, why, recorded, handed_on}] — EVERY FILED
#       VERDICT, with the sha256 of its bytes, whether it counts and whether a record
#       took it: the same file is printed with the same hash before and after that
#       record · view: {file, sha256, on_disk}, the stop's view · attempts: {begun,
#       unrecorded} · held: [{name, kind, state, verdict}] — THE HELD COMMANDS under the
#       scratch root, as their own files say (judged: its verdict is kept; ended: the
#       command has ended and no step has asked after it since, so no verdict is kept;
#       no-exit: it runs, or its supervisor is gone) · trail: [{commit, subject,
#       attempts}] — THE RECORDS MADE, every commit on the opening with the attempts
#       whose marker it took · pending_paths: what `git status` shows, by its path ·
#       acts: [{act, returned, status, next, shows, stands}] (below) · tool: {wrote,
#       reads, same} — the tool that wrote the opening and the one that reads it now,
#       by sha256 · sheet: [{plan, key, class, returns}] — THE ANSWER SHEET · to_answer.
#
# WHAT THE ORCHESTRATOR INVOKES — the line of `setup`, with nothing to interpret:
#
#   session_cwd   THE DIRECTORY THE INVOKING SESSION STANDS IN: the clone. A stage runs
#                 in whatever repository its session stands in, and every agent's shell
#                 starts there.
#   script_path   the harness, BY ITS PATH INSIDE THE CLONE. The Workflow is invoked by
#                 `scriptPath` and never by its name: a workflow invoked by name runs the
#                 copy the session loaded first, which goes stale without a word.
#   args          the `args` object of the FIRST invocation, as it is: the `test` stage,
#                 the run, the scratch root, and a `scope` that names the round's one door.
#   args_then     the `args` object of EVERY LATER invocation: the same without `scope`.
#                 A round's scope is written once, and an invocation that finishes a
#                 triage takes none — the first arguments, sent again, are refused there.
#   returns       the two files under ROOT/results the session writes what each
#                 invocation returned to, as JSON: `first`, and `then`.
#   check         the one command that reads the result: one plain invocation of this
#                 tool, run from this repository's root.
#   expect        how many agents the first invocation launches where every agent ends
#                 well and leaves nothing open, and how many of those are one-command
#                 steps of dev/stabilize-step — counted, and held to a simulated stage by
#                 this tool's suite; and the plan's estimate of the wall time in minutes
#                 for the five default items, which nobody has measured (null for any
#                 other set).
#
# THE STEPS OF THE SMALL CANARY, each one plain invocation:
#
#   1. A root, outside the repository:
#        mktemp -d /ABSOLUTE/SCRATCH/canary.XXXXXX
#   2. The setup, from this repository's root, with the path step 1 printed:
#        dev/stabilize-canary setup --root ROOT --commit HEAD --run canary-small --previous VERSION --previous-commit SHA
#   3. The session, STANDING IN `session_cwd`, invokes the Workflow with `scriptPath` =
#      `script_path` and `args` = `args`, and writes what it returned to `returns.first`.
