# WHAT `check` SAYS OF EACH ACT (`acts`), so that a part is read from the run and from no
# memory of a session. TWO THINGS ARE ASKED OF AN ACT. `returned`: its return file is
# there — with the `status` and the `next` it holds, where it holds one JSON object.
# `shows`: THE RUN ITSELF SHOWS THE ACT — by what that act alone leaves, as the parts are
# run in their order from one root: part-1, an attempt that reached no record before the
# stage's first record; part-2, that record; part-3-act-1, another order than the
# opening's; part-3-act-2, a record of filed verdicts; part-4-act-1, a budget above the
# opening's; part-4-act-2, after the record that raised it, an attempt that reached no
# record; part-4-act-4, after it, a record that took an attempt's marker. A LOOK LEAVES
# NOTHING, and the record an ended act left is finished by an invocation that begins no
# attempt: of part-4-act-3 and part-4-act-5 the run shows nothing either way (null). In a
# root opened afresh for a later part, read `trail` and `attempts` — the acts' words are
# the main line's. `stands` is the one word the two make:
#
#   returned          the file is there, and the run shows the act — or cannot show one
#   not-written       the run shows the act and no file is there: IT RAN, AND ITS RETURN
#                     WAS NOT WRITTEN DOWN — or the human ended it, which is right for
#                     part-4-act-2
#   never-ran         neither
#   returned-unshown  a file is there, and the run shows nothing of the act: a refusal, a
#                     halt before anything was left — or a file somebody wrote
#   cannot-tell       no file is there, and the act is one the run cannot show
#
# `to_answer` names what a part's reading — of the transcripts, which no tool here reads —
# has to answer beside the plan's own table: whether a verifier read the answers.
#
# WHAT IT SHOWS (the plan's list): that a stage reaches its record and pushes it with
# real agents — the attempt's marker, the scope, the instruments, triage, a verifier, the
# record's own gate, the one commit, the vetted push, the state read back — and what the
# next invocation answers; the preflight building a commit that is not checked out, the
# previous release's; a reviewer asserting the binary's hash on a pass that drives
# nothing; the long commands held by real agents, and whether a permission prompt
# appears; and, IF the session can stop a subagent, HOW AN AGENT REALLY ENDS: the seeded
# row's verifier is stopped from outside each time it is tried — the canary's ONE plant,
# which is an act of whoever runs the session and nothing this tool seeds — the finding
# stays unverified, `next` is `triage`, and the invocation with `args_then` is the lap
# that builds the commit the round tested.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT SHOW, deliberately: a state or a payload at a real run's size; a push
# that fails, a red candidate, a report the script refuses, a second triage pass, two
# agents that die, a stage killed as a whole; a ruling, a go, a re-run; the check that
# reads CI, which is void in a clone; a cross-model pass; the real remote's rules, the
# real denylist's reach, the real checkout's permission state; any round after the first.
# AND WHAT CAN MAKE IT SHOW LESS: a scope step that writes the door in other words than
# `args.scope` names it leaves the review row unselected and the seeded finding outside
# the round's test set — the state's `never_selected` and `check`'s `selected` say so.
#
# WHAT IT STANDS ON. The opening is written for the scripts and the harness as they
# stand since the second repair plan's K11: NO AGENT OF A STAGE COMPOSES A SHELL. The two
# binaries, the candidate's gate, the regression set and the record's gate are each ONE
# long command dev/stabilize-step holds — started by one plain call of a step, asked
# after by one plain call of another, which its agent runs again while the tool says
# `running`, and judged by the tool. Every report, the round's scope and the record's
# batch reach their writer from a file. So `expect` counts, beside the agents of the
# chains, TWO STEPS PER HELD COMMAND — and the suite's simulated stage goes red where the
# harness launches another number. `args` is the stage's argument object. WHAT THE
# SESSION NEEDS, beside this line: an allow rule for each of the three tools a prompt of
# the harness names a command of — `Bash(dev/stabilize-step *)`,
# `Bash(dev/stabilize-record *)` and, for a probe, `Bash(dev/stabilize-probe *)`. THIS
# TOOL WRITES NONE: a clone carries the settings the commit it was made of holds, so the
# rules travel into it where that commit's .claude/settings.json has them and are the
# session's own to add where it has not — without one, a step's first call of a tool
# asks for a permission. What a role runs of its own craft (a reviewer's rig, a driver's
# commands on the binary, the trial image) is under the session's permission mode
# either way, and a prompt can appear there.
#
# TO DISPOSE OF IT. Nothing is to be removed inside this repository: the tool writes no
# file of it. The canary is ROOT, a directory under a temp or scratch directory, and goes
# when that does; a root is never reused.
#
# The line. One JSON object, printable ASCII, `act` first, and LAST a `sha256` of the
# line as it stands without that field — as dev/stabilize-step prints its line. A refusal
# prints its line too: {act, status: halted, refused: WORD, halt: {root_cause, evidence,
# tree_state, recommendation}}.
#
# Exit status, and the word a refusal opens with — `stabilize-canary: refused <word>: …`,
# one line on stderr:
#
#    0  the act is done
#    2  usage         an unknown act or flag, a missing argument, a value outside its
#                     grammar — a root that is not absolute or not of plain segments, an
#                     item this tool has no row for
#    3  inside        the root lies inside the repository
#    4  no-root       the root's parent is not there, or the root is no directory
#    5  exists        the root holds something already: a root is used once
#    6  taken         the repository holds the run, or its loop branch
#    7  no-commit     the candidate, or the previous release's commit, is no commit of
#                     the repository
#    8  unfit         the commit named lacks a script of a run, or the harness — or,
#                     under --parts, its log does not hold exactly one entry that states
#                     the exit rule
#    9  tool          a command the act runs failed: git, or the clone's own
#                     dev/stabilize-record or dev/stabilize-step — a scanner that could
#                     not run among them — or the file system refused a directory or a
#                     file. Of a setup: the root then holds no canary. Of a `check`:
#                     the canary stands as it stood, and no setup is asked for
#   10  not-isolated  the clone could reach something that is not its bare remote
#   11  not-fresh     the run the setup opened is not in the state a fresh run has —
#                     or, under --parts, a row of it is left for a triage
#   12  missing       the root holds no canary
#   13  link          a directory of the canary is a link, or is not where it should be
#
# Needs python3 and git, and — in the clone, for every write of the opening — what
# dev/stabilize-record needs: gitleaks and a denylist. It finds its repository from where
# it lies, never from where it was called, and it writes nothing inside it.
# tooling-tests/dev_stabilize_canary.rs drives it.

